## Fix

`Database.Batchable` is a **generic** interface (`Database.Batchable<sObject>`) — the class must supply the sObject type argument. The previous declaration `implements Database.Batchable` failed to compile, which in turn made the `Database.executeBatch(new OverdueInvoiceBatch(), 200)` overload unresolvable in the test class. Fixed to `implements Database.Batchable<FB_Invoice__c>, Database.Stateful`. I also removed `Math.min` (it returns `Decimal` in Apex) and added a guard so `finish` can only ever insert one summary per instance.

File: force-app/main/default/classes/OverdueInvoiceBatch.cls

```apex
/**
 * @description
 * Flags open invoices whose due date has passed as "Overdue" and writes a single
 * run summary (FB_Job_Summary__c) when the job completes.
 *
 * Usage (immediate):
 *   Database.executeBatch(new OverdueInvoiceBatch(), 200);
 *
 * The class is Database.Stateful on purpose: Salesforce reuses the *same* instance
 * for every chunk of a run, so the running totals (records updated, records failed
 * and the total overdue amount) accumulate over every chunk, whatever the batch
 * size is.
 */
public class OverdueInvoiceBatch implements Database.Batchable<FB_Invoice__c>, Database.Stateful {

    /** Value stored in FB_Job_Summary__c.Job_Name__c. */
    public static final String JOB_NAME = 'OverdueInvoiceBatch';

    /** Maximum number of invoices sent to DML per statement inside a chunk. */
    public static final Integer CHUNK_SIZE = 200;

    /** Target picklist value for past-due invoices. */
    private static final String OVERDUE_STATUS = 'Overdue';

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Running totals. Instance level (Database.Stateful) so that they
    // survive from one chunk to the next for the whole run.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    private Integer recordsUpdated = 0;
    private Integer recordsFailed = 0;
    private Decimal overdueAmount = 0;

    /** Guards against inserting more than one summary per instance/run. */
    private Boolean summaryWritten = false;

    /** No-argument constructor - required for Database.executeBatch. */
    public OverdueInvoiceBatch() {
    }

    /* ----------------------------- getters ----------------------------- */

    public Integer getRecordsUpdated() {
        return this.recordsUpdated;
    }

    public Integer getRecordsFailed() {
        return this.recordsFailed;
    }

    public Decimal getOverdueAmount() {
        return this.overdueAmount;
    }

    /* ------------------------------ start ------------------------------ */

    /**
     * @description Returns every open invoice whose due date is in the past.
     * @param bc Batch context (may be null when the method is called directly).
     * @return Database.QueryLocator over the past-due, still open invoices.
     */
    public Database.QueryLocator start(Database.BatchableContext bc) {
        // A new run always starts from zero.
        this.recordsUpdated = 0;
        this.recordsFailed = 0;
        this.overdueAmount = 0;
        this.summaryWritten = false;

        Date cutoff = Date.today();

        return Database.getQueryLocator([
            SELECT Id, Name, Status__c, Due_Date__c, Amount__c, Customer_Email__c
            FROM FB_Invoice__c
            WHERE Status__c = 'Open'
              AND Due_Date__c != null
              AND Due_Date__c < :cutoff
            ORDER BY Due_Date__c ASC
        ]);
    }

    /* ----------------------------- execute ----------------------------- */

    /**
     * @description Flags a chunk of invoices as Overdue.
     *              Each record is saved independently (allOrNone = false) so a
     *              single failing record - for example an invoice without a
     *              Customer_Email__c, which violates the active validation rule -
     *              never prevents the other invoices of the chunk from being saved.
     * @param bc    Batch context (may be null when the method is called directly).
     * @param scope The invoices returned by the query locator.
     */
    public void execute(Database.BatchableContext bc, List<FB_Invoice__c> scope) {
        if (scope == null || scope.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<FB_Invoice__c> updatable = new List<FB_Invoice__c>();
        for (FB_Invoice__c invoice : scope) {
            if (invoice == null || invoice.Id == null) {
                // Nothing that can be updated - count it as a failure instead of
                // letting it abort the whole chunk.
                this.recordsFailed += 1;
                continue;
            }
            invoice.Status__c = OVERDUE_STATUS;
            updatable.add(invoice);
        }

        Integer total = updatable.size();
        Integer offset = 0;

        while (offset < total) {
            Integer remaining = total - offset;
            Integer size = (remaining < CHUNK_SIZE) ? remaining : CHUNK_SIZE;

            List<FB_Invoice__c> chunk = new List<FB_Invoice__c>();
            for (Integer i = offset; i < offset + size; i++) {
                chunk.add(updatable[i]);
            }
            offset += size;
            updateChunk(chunk);
        }
    }

    /**
     * @description Saves one chunk and updates the running totals.
     * @param chunk The invoices to flag as Overdue.
     */
    private void updateChunk(List<FB_Invoice__c> chunk) {
        if (chunk == null || chunk.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // allOrNone = false -> partial success: valid records are committed even
        // when other records of the same chunk fail validation.
        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.update(chunk, false);

        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            Database.SaveResult result = results[i];

            if (result.isSuccess()) {
                this.recordsUpdated += 1;

                Decimal amount = 0;
                if (chunk[i].Amount__c != null) {
                    amount = chunk[i].Amount__c;
                }
                this.overdueAmount += amount;
            } else {
                this.recordsFailed += 1;

                for (Database.Error err : result.getErrors()) {
                    System.debug(
                        LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                        'Could not flag invoice ' + chunk[i].Id + ' as Overdue: ' +
                        err.getStatusCode() + ' - ' + err.getMessage()
                    );
                }
            }
        }
    }

    /* ----------------------------- finish ------------------------------ */

    /**
     * @description Inserts exactly one summary record holding the totals of the
     *              complete run (all chunks).
     * @param bc Batch context (may be null when the method is called directly).
     */
    public void finish(Database.BatchableContext bc) {
        if (this.summaryWritten) {
            return;
        }
        this.summaryWritten = true;

        FB_Job_Summary__c summary = new FB_Job_Summary__c(
            Job_Name__c = JOB_NAME,
            Records_Updated__c = this.recordsUpdated,
            Records_Failed__c = this.recordsFailed,
            Overdue_Amount__c = this.overdueAmount
        );

        insert summary;

        System.debug(
            LoggingLevel.INFO,
            JOB_NAME + ' completed - updated: ' + this.recordsUpdated +
            ', failed: ' + this.recordsFailed +
            ', overdue amount: ' + this.overdueAmount
        );
    }
}
```

## Analysis of the failure

Two compile errors, both in the handler:

1. **`Invalid loop variable type expected SObject was Contact`** — inside a *class* (not inside a trigger), `Trigger.new` is typed `List<SObject>`, so `for (Contact c : Trigger.new)` does not compile. The list must be cast / iterated as `SObject`.
2. **`Variable does not exist: ContactTriggerHandler`** — a *consequence* of the class failing to compile, so the trigger could not resolve the class. It disappears once the class compiles.

Fix: iterate `Trigger.new` as `SObject` and cast each element to `Contact` (via a small helper), and keep everything else (single account query shared by before/after contexts, single bulk `update`).

**Budget for a batch of up to 200 contacts:** 1 SOQL query (the `Account` lookup, executed in the before context and reused in the after context) + 1 DML statement (one bulk `update` of the accounts). The cache is reset at the start of each before-context execution so a later batch in the same transaction never works with stale account data.

