/**
 * AccountTrigger (after insert)
 * ============================
 *
 * Pushes every newly created Account to the ERP as a customer.
 *
 * Design
 * ------
 * 1. The trigger runs once per transaction, and Trigger.new then contains ALL
 *    accounts created in that transaction (1 account for an interactive create,
 *    up to 200 for the marketplace integration). The complete list is handed to
 *    ErpCustomerSync - the trigger never looks at Trigger.new[0] only - so no
 *    account is silently skipped.
 *
 * 2. The ERP is called directly from the trigger (one HTTP request per
 *    account, body = JSON array with one element), so the user immediately
 *    sees an error if the ERP does not accept the account. For this to work
 *    the helper class ErpCustomerSync implements Database.AllowsCallouts -
 *    without that interface the platform rejects the callout with
 *    "Callout from triggers are currently not supported".
 *
 * 3. DEVIATION (documented on purpose): a Salesforce transaction may contain
 *    at most 100 callouts. The marketplace integration creates up to 200
 *    accounts in one transaction, so pushing all of them synchronously from
 *    this trigger would raise System.LimitException ("Too many callouts: 101")
 *    and roll the whole insert back. Transactions with more than 100 accounts
 *    are therefore handed over to asynchronous jobs
 *    (ErpCustomerSync.ErpCustomerSyncJob) - one job per block of at most 100
 *    accounts, each job still sending exactly one request per account. The
 *    jobs are enqueued independently (no job enqueues another job), which
 *    keeps the execution flat and avoids recursive job execution.
 *
 * 4. There is no DML in or after the callout path, which keeps us clear of
 *    the "You have uncommitted work pending" error that occurs when DML
 *    follows a callout in the same transaction.
 */
trigger AccountTrigger on Account (after insert) {
    if (Trigger.isAfter && Trigger.isInsert) {
        ErpCustomerSync.pushAccounts(Trigger.new);
    }
}
