```apex
/**
 * ErpCustomerSync
 * ===============
 *
 * Pushes newly created Accounts to the ERP as customers.
 *
 * Endpoint (Named Credential "ERP", already configured in the org):
 *   POST callout:ERP/api/customers
 *   Content-Type: application/json
 *
 * Body - the endpoint expects an array of 1..200 customers. We always send
 * exactly one customer per request so that the ERP team can trace every
 * account on its own:
 *   [{"salesforceId":"001...","name":"Acme GmbH","country":"Germany"}]
 *
 * "salesforceId" is the 18 character Id of the account, "name" the account
 * name and "country" the BillingCountry (null when it is blank).
 *
 * The ERP answers 202 Accepted. Retries and further error handling are out of
 * scope of the ticket; a non 202 answer aborts the transaction so that a
 * rejected customer is not silently dropped.
 *
 * No secrets are stored in this class: endpoint, authentication and the
 * certificate/OAuth configuration live in the Named Credential.
 *
 * NOTE: the class implements Database.AllowsCallouts. That interface is
 * mandatory, because the callout is made from AccountTrigger - without it the
 * platform fails with "Callout from triggers are currently not supported".
 */
public with sharing class ErpCustomerSync implements Database.AllowsCallouts {

    /** Customer endpoint of the ERP, served through the Named Credential "ERP". */
    private static final String ENDPOINT = 'callout:ERP/api/customers';

    /** Socket timeout for a single ERP call. */
    private static final Integer CALL_TIMEOUT_MS = 20000;

    /** The ERP acknowledges an accepted customer with 202 Accepted. */
    private static final Integer HTTP_ACCEPTED = 202;

    /** Maximum length of the ERP error body that is copied into the message. */
    private static final Integer MAX_ERROR_BODY_LENGTH = 500;

    /**
     * Platform limit: maximum number of callouts allowed per transaction.
     * Used as block size for the asynchronous fallback, so that one job never
     * exceeds the limit.
     */
    public static final Integer MAX_CALLOUTS_PER_TRANSACTION = 100;

    /**
     * One ERP customer. Public instance variables so that the payload can be
     * inspected / serialized easily.
     */
    public class ErpCustomer {
        public String salesforceId;
        public String name;
        public String country;

        public ErpCustomer(String salesforceId, String name, String country) {
            this.salesforceId = salesforceId;
            this.name = name;
            this.country = country;
        }
    }

    /**
     * Entry point used by AccountTrigger: pushes the given accounts to the ERP.
     *
     * @param accounts newly created accounts (Trigger.new)
     */
    public static void pushAccounts(List<Account> accounts) {
        if (accounts == null || accounts.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<ErpCustomer> customers = new List<ErpCustomer>();
        for (Account account : accounts) {
            customers.add(toErpCustomer(account));
        }

        pushCustomers(customers);
    }

    /**
     * Sends one HTTP request per customer.
     *
     * Up to MAX_CALLOUTS_PER_TRANSACTION customers are sent synchronously (that
     * is the normal case: the callout happens right inside the trigger). If the
     * transaction contains more accounts than a transaction is allowed to call
     * out, the customers are spread over several asynchronous jobs - still one
     * request per customer.
     */
    public static void pushCustomers(List<ErpCustomer> customers) {
        if (customers == null || customers.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        if (customers.size() > MAX_CALLOUTS_PER_TRANSACTION) {
            dispatchAsync(customers);
            return;
        }

        for (ErpCustomer customer : customers) {
            sendCustomer(customer);
        }
    }

    /**
     * Splits the customers into blocks of at most MAX_CALLOUTS_PER_TRANSACTION
     * and enqueues one job per block.
     *
     * The jobs are enqueued independently - a job never enqueues another job -
     * so the execution stays flat (no recursive job execution) and every job
     * runs in its own transaction with its own callout budget.
     */
    public static void dispatchAsync(List<ErpCustomer> customers) {
        Integer total = customers.size();
        Integer offset = 0;

        while (offset < total) {
            List<ErpCustomer> block = new List<ErpCustomer>();
            while (offset < total && block.size() < MAX_CALLOUTS_PER_TRANSACTION) {
                block.add(customers[offset]);
                offset++;
            }
            System.enqueueJob(new ErpCustomerSyncJob(block));
        }
    }

    /**
     * Sends one customer to the ERP: a single POST request whose body is a
     * JSON array containing exactly one element.
     */
    public static void sendCustomer(ErpCustomer customer) {
        HttpRequest request = new HttpRequest();
        request.setMethod('POST');
        request.setEndpoint(ENDPOINT);
        request.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
        request.setHeader('Accept', 'application/json');
        request.setTimeout(CALL_TIMEOUT_MS);
        request.setBody(toJson(customer));

        HttpResponse response = new Http().send(request);

        if (response.getStatusCode() != HTTP_ACCEPTED) {
            throw new CalloutException(
                'ERP did not accept customer ' + customer.salesforceId
                + ' (HTTP ' + response.getStatusCode() + '): ' + summarize(response.getBody())
            );
        }
    }

    /**
     * Maps an Account onto the ERP payload.
     * The 18 character account Id is sent as salesforceId, the account name as
     * name and a blank BillingCountry is sent as null.
     */
    private static ErpCustomer toErpCustomer(Account account) {
        String country = String.isBlank(account.BillingCountry) ? null : account.BillingCountry.trim();
        return new ErpCustomer(account.Id, account.Name, country);
    }

    /**
     * Builds the request body.
     *
     * The JSON is written explicitly (instead of JSON.serialize on the object)
     * for two reasons:
     * - the field order stays exactly as agreed with the ERP
     *   (salesforceId, name, country), and
     * - a null country is sent as an explicit "country":null. The Apex
     *   serializer omits null attributes, which would change the payload the
     *   ERP expects.
     * The string values themselves are serialized with JSON.serialize so that
     * quotes, backslashes and line breaks are escaped correctly.
     */
    private static String toJson(ErpCustomer customer) {
        StringBuilder json = new StringBuilder();
        json.append('[');
        json.append('{');
        json.append('"salesforceId":').append(JSON.serialize(customer.salesforceId));
        json.append(',"name":').append(JSON.serialize(customer.name));
        json.append(',"country":');
        if (customer.country == null) {
            json.append('null');
        } else {
            json.append(JSON.serialize(customer.country));
        }
        json.append('}');
        json.append(']');
        return json.toString();
    }

    /** Shortens the ERP response body for use in the exception message. */
    private static String summarize(String body) {
        if (body == null) {
            return '';
        }
        if (body.length() > MAX_ERROR_BODY_LENGTH) {
            return body.substring(0, MAX_ERROR_BODY_LENGTH) + '...';
        }
        return body;
    }

    /**
     * Asynchronous counterpart of pushCustomers().
     *
     * Used when one transaction created more accounts than the platform allows
     * callouts for (100, e.g. the marketplace integration with up to 200
     * accounts). One job handles at most MAX_CALLOUTS_PER_TRANSACTION
     * customers and sends exactly one request per customer.
     */
    public class ErpCustomerSyncJob implements Queueable, Database.AllowsCallouts {

        private final List<ErpCustomer> customers;

        public ErpCustomerSyncJob(List<ErpCustomer> customers) {
            this.customers = customers;
        }

        public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
            for (ErpCustomer customer : customers) {
                sendCustomer(customer);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**What I changed and why (deviations)**

1. **`Database.AllowsCallouts` on `ErpCustomerSync` (and on the job class).** This is the fix for `System.CalloutException: Callout from triggers are currently not supported.` A helper class that is invoked from a trigger and performs a callout *must* implement `Database.AllowsCallouts`; the interface is now implemented, so the callout really is made right from the trigger, one request per account, as the ticket requires.
2. **No chaining of jobs.** The previous version enqueued a job that enqueued another job; in the test context that led to `System.AsyncException: Maximum stack depth has been reached`. The blocks are now enqueued independently from the trigger, so job execution is flat.
3. **Batches of more than 100 accounts go asynchronous** (`ErpCustomerSyncJob`, one block per ≤ 100 accounts, one request per account). A transaction may contain at most 100 callouts, so a single synchronous insert of 200 accounts would fail with `System.LimitException: Too many callouts: 101` and roll the insert back. The 1 account → 1 ERP request mapping is unchanged, and for the normal case (single account, or any batch ≤ 100) the request is still sent synchronously from the trigger.
4. **Payload built explicitly**, so the field order is `salesforceId, name, country` and a blank `BillingCountry` is sent as an explicit `"country": null` (Apex's `JSON.serialize` would drop null attributes).
5. **Non-202 responses raise a `CalloutException`**, so a rejected customer is not silently dropped; retries/back-off remain out of scope as agreed.

File: force-app/main/default/classes/FollowUpTaskService.cls

```apex
/**
 * FollowUpTaskService
 *
 * Creates one follow-up Task per Opportunity coming out of the weekly pipeline review.
 *
 * Contract:
 *   createFollowUps(opportunityIds)
 *     - creates exactly one Task per Opportunity:
 *           WhatId       = Opportunity Id
 *           OwnerId      = Opportunity owner
 *           Subject      = 'Follow up: ' + Opportunity.Name
 *           ActivityDate = today + 7 days
 *           Status       = 'Not Started'
 *           Priority     = 'Normal'
 *     - a Task rejected by a validation rule / trigger / flow must NOT prevent the other
 *       Tasks from being created (partial success, per-record error isolation)
 *     - returns the Ids of the Opportunities whose Task could NOT be created
 *       (empty set when every Task was created)
 *
 * Bulk notes (this method is called with 150-250 Opportunities in a single call):
 *   - ONE SOQL query and ONE DML statement are used for the whole batch, because the
 *     per-transaction limits are 100 SOQL queries and 150 DML statements. A per-record
 *     loop would hit "Too many DML statements" at 150+ records and "Too many SOQL queries"
 *     at 100+ records.
 *   - The single DML statement uses allOrNone = false (partial success), which gives the
 *     same isolation semantics as a per-record try/catch (one rejected record does not
 *     roll back the others) and additionally captures record-level trigger/flow errors
 *     that an all-or-none insert would turn into a full transaction rollback.
 */
public with sharing class FollowUpTaskService {

    private static final String SUBJECT_PREFIX = 'Follow up: ';
    private static final Integer DUE_DATE_OFFSET_DAYS = 7;
    private static final String STATUS_NOT_STARTED = 'Not Started';
    private static final String PRIORITY_NORMAL = 'Normal';
    private static final String OPPORTUNITY_SOBJECT_TYPE = 'Opportunity';

    /**
     * @param opportunityIds Ids of the Opportunities reviewed in the pipeline meeting.
     * @return Ids of the Opportunities for which a follow-up Task could not be created.
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();

        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 0. Guard + clean the input
        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // De-duplicate (the same Opportunity can be listed twice) and drop nulls.
        Set<Id> requestedIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id requestedId : opportunityIds) {
            if (requestedId != null) {
                requestedIds.add(requestedId);
            }
        }
        if (requestedIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 1. Only Opportunity Ids can be queried against Opportunity.
        //    Binding a non-Opportunity Id (Account/Lead/Contact/garbage) into an
        //    Opportunity Id filter raises a query error that would kill the whole
        //    batch, so those Ids are routed straight to the "failed" result.
        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        Set<Id> candidateIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id requestedId : requestedIds) {
            if (isOpportunityId(requestedId)) {
                candidateIds.add(requestedId);
            } else {
                failedOpportunityIds.add(requestedId);
            }
        }
        if (candidateIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 2. One bulk query for Name + OwnerId (one query per Opportunity would
        //    exceed the 100 SOQL statements limit).
        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        Map<Id, Opportunity> opportunitiesById;
        try {
            opportunitiesById = new Map<Id, Opportunity>([
                SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId
                FROM Opportunity
                WHERE Id IN :candidateIds
                WITH USER_MODE
            ]);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // We cannot read the Opportunities, so no follow-up Task can be created.
            System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                'FollowUpTaskService: Opportunity query failed: ' + e.getMessage());
            failedOpportunityIds.addAll(candidateIds);
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // Requested Opportunities we cannot read (deleted, no access, filtered by
        // sharing) can never get a Task -> report them as failures.
        for (Id candidateId : candidateIds) {
            if (!opportunitiesById.containsKey(candidateId)) {
                failedOpportunityIds.add(candidateId);
            }
        }

        if (opportunitiesById.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 3. Build one Task per Opportunity
        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        Date dueDate = Date.today().addDays(DUE_DATE_OFFSET_DAYS);

        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        for (Opportunity opportunity : opportunitiesById.values()) {
            tasks.add(new Task(
                WhatId = opportunity.Id,
                OwnerId = opportunity.OwnerId,
                Subject = SUBJECT_PREFIX + opportunity.Name,
                ActivityDate = dueDate,
                Status = STATUS_NOT_STARTED,
                Priority = PRIORITY_NORMAL
            ));
        }

        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 4. Single partial-success DML statement for the whole batch.
        //    allOrNone = false -> a rejected Task does not roll back the others.
        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        List<Database.SaveResult> results;
        try {
            results = Database.insert(tasks, false);
        } catch (DmlException e) {
            // Transaction-level failure (fatal trigger/flow error, limit hit, etc.):
            // nothing was committed, so every Opportunity we tried is a failure.
            System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                'FollowUpTaskService: batch insert failed, no Tasks were created: ' + e.getMessage());
            for (Task task : tasks) {
                failedOpportunityIds.add(task.WhatId);
            }
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 5. Map per-record failures back to their Opportunity (results are index
        //    aligned with the tasks list).
        // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            Database.SaveResult result = results[i];
            if (result.isSuccess()) {
                continue;
            }

            Id opportunityId = tasks[i].WhatId;
            failedOpportunityIds.add(opportunityId);

            for (Database.Error recordError : result.getErrors()) {
                System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                    'FollowUpTaskService: follow-up Task for Opportunity ' + opportunityId +
                    ' was rejected [' + recordError.getStatusCode() + ']: ' + recordError.getMessage());
            }
        }

        return failedOpportunityIds;
    }

    /**
     * True only for Ids that really point at an Opportunity. Guards against
     * non-Opportunity or malformed Ids being bound into the Opportunity query.
     */
    private static Boolean isOpportunityId(Id recordId) {
        try {
            // Id.getSObjectType() returns a Schema.DescribeSObjectResult,
            // so the check is done on the SObject type name.
            String sObjectTypeName = recordId.getSObjectType().getName();
            return OPPORTUNITY_SOBJECT_TYPE.equals(sObjectTypeName);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Malformed Id string -> not a queryable Opportunity Id.
            return false;
        }
    }
}
```

### Fixes applied

1. **`AuraEnabled` removed from `createFollowUps`.** `@AuraEnabled` does not support `Set<Id>` as a return type (Aura-enabled methods must return primitives, Strings, or JSON-serializable collections such as `List<String>`/`List<Map>`), which is what broke the dependent test class. The required signature `public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds)` is unchanged and stays a plain Apex static method, callable from Apex, Flow (via an Apex invocable action), and the test class. If a UI/LWC caller is ever needed, add a *separate* wrapper with a supported signature, e.g. `@AuraEnabled(cacheable=false) public static List<String> createFollowUpsForUi(List<String> ids)` that converts the Strings to Ids, calls this method and returns the failed Opportunity Ids as Strings — no change to the core method's contract.

2. **`Id.getSObjectType()` comparison corrected.** `Id.getSObjectType()` returns a `Schema.DescribeSObjectResult`, not a `Schema.SObjectType`, so comparing it directly to `Schema.SObjectType.Opportunity` is a compile error. The helper now calls `.getName()` and compares against `'Opportunity'`, keeping the same intent (reject Ids that do not point at an Opportunity before they are bound into the `Opportunity` query, and report them as failures).

Everything else from the previous answer is unchanged: one bulk `SELECT` plus one `Database.insert(tasks, false)` (partial success, so a rejected Task never stops the others), per-record errors mapped back to their Opportunity by index alignment, `try/catch` as a safety net around the batch DML, and the return value is the set of Opportunity Ids whose Task could not be created (empty when all succeeded).

The failure was caused by `List.subList()`, which does not exist in Apex (it's a Java/`.NET` API). My chunking helper has been rewritten with a plain index loop, and I removed the local variable that shadowed the helper method name. The trigger error was a knock-on effect of the class failing to compile, so `AccountRatingService` was not in the org when the trigger was compiled.

