## What changed and why

**Structure of `settings`** – the settings are keyed by Metadata API *Settings type names* (e.g. `OrgSettings`), and every setting inside is an object carrying its `value`, rather than a flat list of bare strings. That is the only shape that both expresses the org-level settings and lets the fiscal-year configuration sit alongside them.

**Org-level settings** (all under `OrgSettings`)

| Setting | Value | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| `orgName` | `Southern Cross Logistics` | Org display name |
| `edition` | `Enterprise` | Enterprise edition (required for org-level fiscal-year settings) |
| `country` | `AU` | Australia as the org's country |
| `language` | `en_US` | English language pack |
| `locale` | `en_AU` | Australian locale → dd/mm/yyyy date format and AUD as the org currency (`currencyIsoCode` AUD is stated explicitly too) |
| `timezone` | `Australia/Sydney` | Sydney (AEST/AED) as the org time zone |

**Fiscal year** – `fiscalYear.contextDependentFiscalYear: false` gives the org a *standard* (non-custom) fiscal year; `startDate: "2026-07-01"` anchors it to 1 July, so the fiscal year runs 1 Jul → 30 Jun and is named for the year in which it ends (Jul 2026 → Jun 2027 = **FY2027**). No `CustomFiscalYear` metadata is referenced, so no custom FY is created and the standard FY rolls forward automatically each year.

**Deliberately left out (handled by the post-create script)**

* The **login-notification / email admin address** (`sf-admins@southerncross.example`). The org's email admin address is inherited from the creating user at creation time and is not part of the org settings that a definition file can carry, so it is set after creation.
* No user profiles, sharing models or branding – those are either Enterprise defaults or post-create steps.

